data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0b13658f32f5c250006a5015d9132c8193bfb371cb993ea60a","logprobs":[],"output_index":5,"sequence_number":352,"text":"Based on available product info, a strong pick under $300 is the SkyView 2‑Person Tent (about $200).【7:1†source】\n\nWhat it includes / key features\n- Sleeps 2 comfortably; 3‑season design.【7:1†source】  \n- Rainfly included; fully waterproof materials and double‑stitched seams for weather protection.【7:1†source】  \n- Two large doors and two vestibules (extra covered storage) — vestibule area ~8 sq ft each.【7:1†source】  \n- Mesh panels for ventilation (reduces condensation) and interior storage pockets/gear loft.【7:1†source】  \n- Freestanding with lightweight aluminum poles; reflective guy lines and stake points for night visibility.【7:1†source】  \n- Packed/ported for transport — packed weight ≈ 8 lbs and comes with a carrying bag.【7:1†source】  \n- Setup: color‑coded poles and simple pitch so it’s quick to assemble. Users report good ventilation, easy setup, and reliable rain protection in practice (some reviewers noted occasional zipper issues).【7:2†source】\n\nQuick pros/cons summary\n- Pros: roomy for two, good ventilation, vestibules for gear, affordable (~$200), easy setup.【7:1†source】【7:2†source】  \n- Cons: a few user reviews mention zipper quality could be better; not a heavy‑duty 4‑season mountaineering tent.【7:2†source】\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- Compare this model to 2–3 other tents under $300 (car‑camping vs backpacking options), or  \n- Recommend the best 2‑person tent specifically for backpacking (priority: weight/packed size) or for car camping (priority: space/comfort).\n\nWhich do you prefer?"}

